771 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fff9c6a5-d7fa-5ed1-9590-bb662eb6e249" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559792 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559792 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139527" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559792 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559792 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $11,$ $9,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559792 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "352fc2e3-10d1-5d70-be93-5831359ed522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559792 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $11,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=11\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{11}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559796 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559796 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559796 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559796 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nR\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}={Q}^{T}\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559796 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a651338-245e-55fb-922d-e222a82f10f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559796 #edb/ref 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $x$ and $b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559892 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559892 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "00649106-db72-5f98-a26b-cf0c8648ce5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559892 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186366636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559893 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559893 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "00b2cd0a-8e39-5570-a66f-846996a620a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559893 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559900 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559900 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a2276bc-3326-51a6-9c69-6590d1bce6ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559900 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186388618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559903 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559903 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "16892fec-26dd-5ef4-928e-f25b0200b0cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559903 #edb/ref 